An individual is entitled to HI under the deemed insured provision beginning with the first month after 6/66 in which the individual:

• files an application;

• is not eligible for HI under the regular insured provision;

• has attained age 65;

• has, if attains age 65 after 1967, not less than 3 QC's for each calendar year elapsing after 1966 and before the year of attainment of age 65 (an individual who attained age 65 before 1968 needs no QCs to be deemed insured for HI);

• is a U.S. resident;

• is a U.S. citizen or an alien lawfully admitted for permanent residence with 5 years continuous U.S. residence immediately preceding the month all other eligibility requirements are met;

• is not covered, or would not be covered currently if enrolled under the Federal Employees Health Benefits Act of 1959; and

• has not been convicted of certain crimes against the U.S.

The first month of eligibility is usually the month the individual attains age 65. However, the first month of eligibility is later than age 65 in the following situations.

1. The person first meets the citizenship/residency requirements after attains age 65;

2. The person's entitlement to monthly RSDI or RRB benefits terminates after age 65 (e.g., because of divorce or remarriage), and does not qualify for HI under the deemed insured provision. An individual's initial enrollment period (IEP) begins 3 months before such first month and continues for 7 months.

